# `issue` Standalone Wire Specification

An `issue` identifies native XAH, an issued currency, or an MPT issuance. Its
standalone payload is self-discriminating and has no outer field header.

## Variant layout

| JSON form | Standalone payload | Decode discriminator |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `{"currency":"XAH"}` | 20-byte zero currency | A zero first 20-byte word ends the issue immediately. An issuer is forbidden. |
| `{"currency":"USD","issuer":"r..."}` | 20-byte currency + 20-byte issuer | A nonzero second word other than `noAccount()` selects a classic issue. Native/non-native currency and account must be consistent. |
| `{"mpt_issuance_id":"<48 hex>"}` | 20-byte MPT issuer + 20-byte `noAccount()` sentinel + 4-byte sequence | The sentinel in the second word selects the 44-byte MPT form. Currency/issuer keys are mutually exclusive with `mpt_issuance_id`. |

The first 20 bytes alone are not a general length prefix: their value selects
whether another 0, 20, or 24 bytes must follow. Full-input consumption is
therefore required to reject a valid short variant with trailing bytes.

**Portability watch:** the MPT sequence leg is copied between the first four
`MPTID` bytes and a native `uint32_t`, while `Serializer::add32` and
`SerialIter::get32` provide big-endian wire I/O. The paired implementation
round-trips on the current platform, but reviewers must compare this path with
`makeMptID`'s explicit endian conversion before changing or porting it.

